course: dictionary
lesson: 3230
title: Entirely: completely, or only
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Entirely» means completely, totally, in every way. When something is entirely one thing, there is nothing else mixed in. «The shape is entirely shaded» means every single part of it is shaded, with no white bits left.
explain: Sometimes «entirely» means only. «The box is made entirely of wood» tells you there is no metal, plastic or glass in it. Ask yourself: is the word saying everything, or only one thing? The sentence will show you which.
explain: Grown-ups often say «not entirely» to be polite. «I am not entirely sure» means you are not completely sure, so a little doubt is left. The word softens what you say, so it does not sound harsh.
fact: Entirely means completely, totally, or in every way.
fact: Entirely can also mean only, as in «made entirely of wood».
fact: «Not entirely» means «not completely»: part is true, but not all of it.
fact: «Not entirely accurate» is a polite way to say false.
fact: Test your meaning by swapping in «completely»: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
exercise: «The shape is entirely shaded.» What does entirely mean here? | *Completely, every part | Faintly, in pale grey | Partly, just a little | Quickly
exercise: «The box is made entirely of wood.» What does entirely tell us? | Made by a carpenter | Wood with some metal too | *Only wood, nothing else | It is a very big box
exercise: «Toad has given up motor-cars entirely.» Which word means the same as entirely? | Partly | Often | Nearly | *Completely
test: «I am not entirely sure.» What does this mean? | *Not completely sure, a little doubt is left | Asleep | Very angry | Certain, with no doubt at all
test: «Ukraine is the largest country entirely in Europe.» What does entirely tell you? | It is very cold | It is in Asia | *Every part of it is in Europe | Only some of it is in Europe
exam: «The Cat's head fades away, and when he comes back it has entirely disappeared.» What does entirely disappeared mean? | Had faded a little | Was painted over | *Had gone completely, not a trace left | Had grown bigger
